Chapter: IRCheck
  cites Codex chapter Build Settings (max-recursion-depth)

 It is opt-in, and it is off in `default-ir-pipeline`. The shipping
 compiler pays nothing for it: no walk, no skip list, no allocation.
 Turn it on when a pass is under suspicion, and it is on for every
 definition in the chapter, which for a self-compile is every function
 in the compiler.

 Three things are checked, and only three.

 **Scope discipline under shadowing.** Every `IrName` must be bound by
 something. The scope walk seeds from the definition's parameters and
 admits binders from `IrLet`, `IrLambda`, match branch patterns
 (including constructor sub-patterns), `IrDoBind`, and handle clause
 parameters and resume names. But this IR legitimately names things
 that no binder introduced -- chapter-level definitions, builtins,
 effect operations, nullary constructors -- and the emitter resolves
 exactly those four when a name misses the locals (`emit-name`:
 locals, then `is-builtin`, then the user-arity table, then the
 handler table, then a nullary constructor tag). So the checker admits
 the same four, and a name is a violation only when it is in none of
 them. **A false positive here is worse than a miss.** Constructors are
 admitted by the name-resolver's own `is-type-name`, because the ctor
 list is not carried on the IRChapter; that is the one place this check
 is coarser than the emitter, and it is coarse in the safe direction.

 **Type presence.** Every expression node, parameter, and definition
 that carries a `CodexType` carries a resolved one. `ErrorTy` there
 means an earlier phase failed silently.

 The contract is not universal, and finding out where it fails was the
 first thing this checker did. **Three places in the IR carry `ErrorTy`
 on perfectly valid programs today**, and all three are excluded from
 the check rather than papered over:

 1. **Constructor sub-patterns** -- `lookup-ctor-field-type` answers
    `ErrorTy` for any scrutinee not already a resolved `SumTy`, which
    is every polymorphic sum. `when b is Wrap (x)` types `x` as
    `ErrorTy`.
 2. **The element type of an empty `IrList`** -- `[]` has no element to
    take a type from.
 3. **Un-annotated lambdas** -- `let f = \a -> a + a in ...` lowers the
    `IrLambda`, its parameters, and the `IrLet` that binds it with
    `ErrorTy`; nothing carries the checker's inferred type down into
    lowering for that node.

 None of the three is a miscompile -- codegen never reads those slots.
 All three are a warning to the middle-end campaign: **a pass may not
 yet reason from a type it reads out of the IR** without knowing which
 of these it is standing on. They are the honest reason the ErrorTy
 check is narrower than "every node".

 **Structural sanity.** A match with no branches, an apply whose
 function position is a literal, a let that binds the empty name.

 Limitations, stated so they are not assumed away: the three type holes
 above are not checked; the ErrorTy check is otherwise shallow (an
 `ErrorTy` nested inside a `ListTy` is not reported); no effect-row
 agreement is checked; no arity checking; a constructor name is
 admitted on its spelling, not against a constructor table; and the
 walk is depth- and scope-bounded, so a pathological tree is reported
 as unchecked rather than walked forever.

Section: Diagnostic Code

 9xxx is the compiler-internal band (CdxCodes: 9001 resource exhausted,
 9002 deck overflow, 9003 escape violation). A malformed IR is the
 compiler's fault, never the program's, so it belongs here and nowhere
 else. The constant lives beside its only producer until CdxCodes can
 be edited to carry the registry entry.

  cdx-ir-check-violation : Integer = 9004

Section: Bounds

 The walk is bounded twice. Depth is the recursion budget every other
 tree walker in the compiler uses, so a cyclic or pathological IR
 cannot hang the checker. Scope is a fixed stack of names: binders are
 written into it by index and the count in hand is the only authority
 on what is in scope, which is what makes shadowing correct -- a sibling
 branch overwrites its predecessor's slots rather than inheriting them.
 The stack is allocated once for the whole chapter and never grows, so
 the scope walk allocates nothing per definition.

 Exhausting either bound is itself reported. The checker never answers
 "clean" about a subtree it did not read.

  ir-check-scope-cap : Integer = 1024

Section: Entry Point

  ir-check : IRChapter -> List Text
  ir-check (ch) =
   let globals = ir-check-globals ch
   in let stack = ir-check-make-stack 0 []
   in ir-check-defs globals stack (ch.defs) 0 (list-length (ch.defs)) []

  ir-check-globals : IRChapter -> SkipListText
  ir-check-globals (ch) =
   let s0 = skip-list-text-from-list builtin-names
   in let s1 = ir-check-add-def-names s0 (ch.defs) 0 (list-length (ch.defs))
   in ir-check-add-names s1 (ch.effect-op-names) 0 (list-length (ch.effect-op-names))

  ir-check-add-def-names : SkipListText, List IRDef, Integer, Integer -> SkipListText
  ir-check-add-def-names (s) (defs) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then s
   else ir-check-add-def-names (skip-list-text-insert s ((list-at defs i).name)) defs (i + 1) len

  ir-check-add-names : SkipListText, List Text, Integer, Integer -> SkipListText
  ir-check-add-names (s) (xs) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then s
   else ir-check-add-names (skip-list-text-insert s (list-at xs i)) xs (i + 1) len

  ir-check-make-stack : Integer, List Text -> List Text
  ir-check-make-stack (i) (acc) =
   if i >= ir-check-scope-cap then acc
   else ir-check-make-stack (i + 1) (list-push acc "")

Section: Environment

  IRCheckEnv = record {
   globals : SkipListText,
   scope : List Text,
   def-name : Text
  }

  ir-check-msg : IRCheckEnv, Text -> Text
  ir-check-msg (env) (m) = (env.def-name) & ": " & m

  ir-check-push : IRCheckEnv, Text, Integer -> Integer
  ir-check-push (env) (n) (nb) =
   if nb >= ir-check-scope-cap then nb
   else let written = list-set-at (env.scope) nb n
   in nb + 1

  ir-check-push-names : IRCheckEnv, List Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ir-check-push-names (env) (xs) (i) (len) (nb) =
   if i >= len then nb
   else ir-check-push-names env xs (i + 1) len (ir-check-push env (list-at xs i) nb)

  ir-check-in-scope : IRCheckEnv, Text, Integer -> Boolean
  ir-check-in-scope (env) (n) (nb) = ir-check-scan (env.scope) n (nb - 1)

  ir-check-scan : List Text, Text, Integer -> Boolean
  ir-check-scan (stack) (n) (i) =
   if i < 0 then False
   else if list-at stack i == n then True
   else ir-check-scan stack n (i - 1)

Section: Predicates

  ir-check-is-error-ty : CodexType -> Boolean
  ir-check-is-error-ty (t) =
   when t
    is ErrorTy -> True
    is otherwise -> False

  ir-check-is-literal : IRExpr -> Boolean
  ir-check-is-literal (e) =
   when e
    is IrIntLit (v) (s) | IrNumLit (v) (s) | IrTextLit (v) (s) | IrBoolLit (v) (s) | IrCharLit (v) (s) -> True
    is otherwise -> False

  ir-check-is-lambda : IRExpr -> Boolean
  ir-check-is-lambda (e) =
   when e
    is IrLambda (ps) (b) (t) (s) -> True
    is otherwise -> False

  ir-check-ty : IRCheckEnv, CodexType, Text, List Text -> List Text
  ir-check-ty (env) (t) (kind) (acc) =
   if ir-check-is-error-ty t then list-push acc (ir-check-msg env (kind & " carries ErrorTy"))
   else acc

Section: Definitions

  ir-check-defs : SkipListText, List Text, List IRDef, Integer, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  ir-check-defs (globals) (stack) (defs) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else ir-check-defs globals stack defs (i + 1) len (ir-check-def globals stack (list-at defs i) acc)

  ir-check-def : SkipListText, List Text, IRDef, List Text -> List Text
  ir-check-def (globals) (stack) (d) (acc) =
   let env = IRCheckEnv { globals = globals, scope = stack, def-name = d.name }
   in let params = d.params
   in let nb = ir-check-bind-params env params 0 (list-length params) 0
   in let acc1 = ir-check-param-types env params 0 (list-length params) acc
   in let acc2 = ir-check-ty env (d.type-val) "definition" acc1
   in ir-check-expr env (d.body) nb 0 acc2

  ir-check-bind-params : IRCheckEnv, List IRParam, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ir-check-bind-params (env) (ps) (i) (len) (nb) =
   if i >= len then nb
   else ir-check-bind-params env ps (i + 1) len (ir-check-push env ((list-at ps i).name) nb)

  ir-check-param-types : IRCheckEnv, List IRParam, Integer, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  ir-check-param-types (env) (ps) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else let p = list-at ps i
   in ir-check-param-types env ps (i + 1) len (ir-check-ty env (p.type-val) ("parameter '" & (p.name) & "'") acc)

Section: Expressions

  ir-check-expr : IRCheckEnv, IRExpr, Integer, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  ir-check-expr (env) (e) (nb) (depth) (acc) =
   if depth >= max-recursion-depth then list-push acc (ir-check-msg env "IR depth budget exhausted; subtree not checked")
   else if nb >= ir-check-scope-cap then list-push acc (ir-check-msg env "scope budget exhausted; subtree not checked")
   else when e
    is IrIntLit (v) (s) | IrNumLit (v) (s) | IrTextLit (v) (s) | IrBoolLit (v) (s) | IrCharLit (v) (s) -> acc
    is IrName (n) (t) (s) ->
     let acc1 = ir-check-ty env t ("IrName '" & n & "'") acc
     in if ir-check-in-scope env n nb then acc1
        else if skip-list-text-has (env.globals) n then acc1
        else if is-type-name n then acc1
        else list-push acc1 (ir-check-msg env ("unbound name '" & n & "'"))
    is IrNegate (x) (t) (s) -> ir-check-expr env x nb (depth + 1) acc
    is IrBinary (op) (l) (r) (t) (s) ->
     let acc1 = ir-check-ty env t "IrBinary" acc
     in let acc2 = ir-check-expr env l nb (depth + 1) acc1
     in ir-check-expr env r nb (depth + 1) acc2
    is IrIf (c) (th) (el) (t) (s) ->
     let acc1 = ir-check-ty env t "IrIf" acc
     in let acc2 = ir-check-expr env c nb (depth + 1) acc1
     in let acc3 = ir-check-expr env th nb (depth + 1) acc2
     in ir-check-expr env el nb (depth + 1) acc3
    is IrLet (n) (t) (v) (b) (s) ->
     let acc1 = if ir-check-is-lambda v then acc else ir-check-ty env t ("IrLet '" & n & "'") acc
     in let acc2 = if n == "" then list-push acc1 (ir-check-msg env "IrLet binds the empty name") else acc1
     in let acc3 = ir-check-expr env v nb (depth + 1) acc2
     in ir-check-expr env b (ir-check-push env n nb) (depth + 1) acc3
    is IrApply (f) (a) (t) (s) ->
     let acc1 = ir-check-ty env t "IrApply" acc
     in let acc2 = if ir-check-is-literal f then list-push acc1 (ir-check-msg env "IrApply applies a literal in function position") else acc1
     in let acc3 = ir-check-expr env f nb (depth + 1) acc2
     in ir-check-expr env a nb (depth + 1) acc3
    is IrLambda (ps) (b) (t) (s) ->
     ir-check-expr env b (ir-check-bind-params env ps 0 (list-length ps) nb) (depth + 1) acc
    is IrList (es) (t) (s) ->
     let acc1 = if list-length es == 0 then acc else ir-check-ty env t "IrList" acc
     in ir-check-exprs env es nb (depth + 1) 0 (list-length es) acc1
    is IrMatch (sc) (bs) (t) (s) ->
     let acc1 = ir-check-ty env t "IrMatch" acc
     in let acc2 = if list-length bs == 0 then list-push acc1 (ir-check-msg env "IrMatch has no branches") else acc1
     in let acc3 = ir-check-expr env sc nb (depth + 1) acc2
     in ir-check-branches env bs nb (depth + 1) 0 (list-length bs) acc3
    is IrAct (ss) (t) (s) ->
     let acc1 = ir-check-ty env t "IrAct" acc
     in ir-check-stmts env ss nb (depth + 1) 0 (list-length ss) acc1
    is IrHandle (eff) (h) (cs) (t) (s) ->
     let acc1 = ir-check-ty env t "IrHandle" acc
     in let acc2 = ir-check-expr env h nb (depth + 1) acc1
     in ir-check-clauses env cs nb (depth + 1) 0 (list-length cs) acc2
    is IrWithTimeout (secs) (effs) (scopes) (body) (t) (s) ->
     let acc1 = ir-check-ty env t "IrWithTimeout" acc
     in ir-check-expr env body nb (depth + 1) acc1
    is IrRecord (n) (fs) (t) (s) ->
     let acc1 = ir-check-ty env t ("IrRecord '" & n & "'") acc
     in ir-check-fields env fs nb (depth + 1) 0 (list-length fs) acc1
    is IrFieldAccess (r) (f) (t) (s) ->
     let acc1 = ir-check-ty env t ("IrFieldAccess '" & f & "'") acc
     in ir-check-expr env r nb (depth + 1) acc1
    is IrFork (b) (t) (s) ->
     let acc1 = ir-check-ty env t "IrFork" acc
     in ir-check-expr env b nb (depth + 1) acc1
    is IrAwait (tk) (t) (s) ->
     let acc1 = ir-check-ty env t "IrAwait" acc
     in ir-check-expr env tk nb (depth + 1) acc1
    is IrTry (mx) (body) (fb) (fail) (t) (s) ->
     let acc1 = ir-check-ty env t "IrTry" acc
     in let acc2 = ir-check-stmts env body nb (depth + 1) 0 (list-length body) acc1
     in let acc3 = ir-check-stmts env fb nb (depth + 1) 0 (list-length fb) acc2
     in ir-check-stmts env fail nb (depth + 1) 0 (list-length fail) acc3
    is IrFieldStore (r) (f) (v) (t) (s) ->
     let acc1 = ir-check-ty env t ("IrFieldStore '" & f & "'") acc
     in let acc2 = ir-check-expr env r nb (depth + 1) acc1
     in ir-check-expr env v nb (depth + 1) acc2
    is IrError (m) (t) (s) -> ir-check-ty env t "IrError" acc

  ir-check-exprs : IRCheckEnv, List IRExpr, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  ir-check-exprs (env) (es) (nb) (depth) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else ir-check-exprs env es nb depth (i + 1) len (ir-check-expr env (list-at es i) nb depth acc)

  ir-check-fields : IRCheckEnv, List IRFieldVal, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  ir-check-fields (env) (fs) (nb) (depth) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else let f = list-at fs i
   in ir-check-fields env fs nb depth (i + 1) len (ir-check-expr env (f.value) nb depth acc)

  ir-check-stmts : IRCheckEnv, List IRActStmt, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  ir-check-stmts (env) (ss) (nb) (depth) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else let s = list-at ss i
   in when s
    is IrDoBind (n) (t) (v) (sp) ->
     let acc1 = ir-check-ty env t ("IrDoBind '" & n & "'") acc
     in let acc2 = ir-check-expr env v nb depth acc1
     in ir-check-stmts env ss (ir-check-push env n nb) depth (i + 1) len acc2
    is IrDoExec (v) (sp) ->
     ir-check-stmts env ss nb depth (i + 1) len (ir-check-expr env v nb depth acc)

  ir-check-branches : IRCheckEnv, List IRBranch, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  ir-check-branches (env) (bs) (nb) (depth) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else let b = list-at bs i
   in let nb2 = ir-check-bind-pat env (b.pattern) nb depth
   in let acc1 = ir-check-expr env (b.guard) nb2 depth acc
   in let acc2 = ir-check-expr env (b.body) nb2 depth acc1
   in ir-check-branches env bs nb depth (i + 1) len acc2

  ir-check-clauses : IRCheckEnv, List IRHandleClause, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  ir-check-clauses (env) (cs) (nb) (depth) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else let c = list-at cs i
   in let nb2 = ir-check-push-names env (c.params) 0 (list-length (c.params)) nb
   in let nb3 = ir-check-push env (c.resume-name) nb2
   in ir-check-clauses env cs nb depth (i + 1) len (ir-check-expr env (c.body) nb3 depth acc)

Section: Patterns

 Patterns are walked for their binders only -- see hole 1 in the chapter
 prose for why their types are not checked. Codegen never reads them
 (it matches on tags and field offsets), so the IR is not wrong, but it
 is not typed either. The check belongs here the day lowering fills
 them in.

  ir-check-bind-pat : IRCheckEnv, IRPat, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ir-check-bind-pat (env) (p) (nb) (depth) =
   if depth >= max-recursion-depth then nb
   else when p
    is IrVarPat (n) (t) (s) -> ir-check-push env n nb
    is IrCtorPat (n) (subs) (t) (s) -> ir-check-bind-pats env subs nb (depth + 1) 0 (list-length subs)
    is IrVecPat (subs) (t) (s) -> ir-check-bind-pats env subs nb (depth + 1) 0 (list-length subs)
    is otherwise -> nb

  ir-check-bind-pats : IRCheckEnv, List IRPat, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ir-check-bind-pats (env) (ps) (nb) (depth) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then nb
   else ir-check-bind-pats env ps (ir-check-bind-pat env (list-at ps i) nb depth) depth (i + 1) len

Section: Reporting

  ir-check-violation-bag : List Text -> DiagnosticBag
  ir-check-violation-bag (vs) =
   ir-check-violation-bag-loop empty-bag vs 0 (list-length vs)

  ir-check-violation-bag-loop : DiagnosticBag, List Text, Integer, Integer -> DiagnosticBag
  ir-check-violation-bag-loop (bag) (vs) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then bag
   else ir-check-violation-bag-loop (bag-add bag (make-error cdx-ir-check-violation (list-at vs i) synthetic-span)) vs (i + 1) len
